    "body": "<!doctype html>\n<!--\n  data-cf-product picks the accent ramp and data-cf-substrate the ash ramp, both from\n  @cloudsforge/ui/tokens.css. They are on <html> and set STATICALLY here rather than by React,\n  because a page that paints before the attributes land flashes the default ember and then\n  changes colour.\n\n  `lantern` is the real registry key (ui/packages/ui/src/surfaces.ts) and it has a\n  DECLARED block at ui/packages/ui/src/tokens.css, so nothing here falls through to the\n  company default in silence. That block carries amber — and this surface then re-points the five\n  accent tokens to the ember ramp in src/styles.css, for the reason tokens.css states\n  verbatim. Naming some other key would have been the lazy way to get ember, and it would have\n  been a lie about which surface this is.\n\n  data-cf-scheme is the third of the three, added with @cloudsforge/ui 1.1. `auto` follows the\n  reader's operating system; `light` and `dark` force one. It is set here, statically, for exactly\n  the reason the other two are — a page that paints before the attribute lands flashes the default\n  and then changes colour, and a theme flash is worse than a theme nobody asked for.\n-->\n<html lang=\"en-GB\" data-cf-product=\"lantern\" data-cf-substrate=\"warm\" data-cf-scheme=\"auto\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\" />\n    <!--\n      `color-scheme`, AND NOT `colour-scheme`, WHICH IS WHAT THIS FILE SHIPPED WITH.\n      ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n      There is no `colour-scheme` meta name in any specification. This estate's copy is British\n      throughout — the routes say \"recognise\", the tokens say \"colour\" — and the spelling leaked\n      into the one string where the language is not English but HTML. Every browser since this\n      surface shipped has parsed the tag, failed to recognise the name and ignored it, silently,\n      because an unknown meta name is not an error anywhere. So the console declared NOTHING about\n      which schemes it supports, which is not the same as declaring dark: the UA was left free to\n      draw its own default form controls and scrollbars over a page that had opinions about both.\n\n      Both schemes are declared, and the token layer is what actually switches them: this line\n      tells the browser which control and scrollbar sets to draw, and `data-cf-scheme` on <html>\n      above tells @cloudsforge/ui which palette to resolve.\n    -->\n    <meta name=\"color-scheme\" content=\"dark light\" />\n    <!--\n      The build that produced this bundle. Stamped by the Dockerfile, read by src/lib/obs.ts, and\n      the reason an error report can be pinned to the deploy that introduced it. It is an identity\n      rather than a configuration: it names the artefact, it does not tell it where it is running.\n    -->\n    <meta name=\"cf-release\" content=\"c13f5a25b707ee404e80c775ab2ac4e9fc1e77d4\" />\n    <!--\n      THE ANALYTICS TAG THAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE, AND THIS IS THE DECISION RATHER THAN THE GAP.\n      ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n      Every public surface in this estate carries a `cf-analytics` meta tag holding a GA4\n      measurement ID, which `@cloudsforge/ui/consent` reads at runtime. THIS SURFACE DOES NOT, AND\n      MUST NOT, and the next reader is meant to find that written down rather than infer it from an\n      absence and helpfully fix it.\n\n      Two facts about this console decide it. It is `adminOnly: true`\n      (ui/packages/ui/src/surfaces.ts), so nobody who is not an operator is on it at all; and\n      its request-lookup route carries the identifier in the QUERY STRING — `/request?id=…`, which\n      `src/lib/routes.ts` explains at length and `nginx.conf` depends on. GA4's default\n      `page_location` parameter is the full URL, query string included. So a single automatic\n      pageview from this page ships a live CloudsForge request id to Google, per lookup, for every\n      incident anybody works — estate identifiers, in a third party's logs, in exchange for\n      knowing how many times an operator opened a table. There is no measurement worth that, and\n      no amount of consent makes it a sensible trade for data that is not the reader's own.\n\n      The consent primitives are still wired below, and that is not an inconsistency: `main.tsx`\n      calls `initAnalytics()` and `components/shell.tsx` renders `<CookieBanner />`, both of which\n      do NOTHING without an ID. `analyticsId()` (ui/packages/ui/src/consent.ts) returns null\n      when the meta tag is absent, `initAnalytics()` then only primes Consent Mode with every\n      category denied — two pushes onto a plain array, no request, no cookie — and the banner\n      returns null rather than asking permission for something that will never happen. The wiring\n      is there so that this file is the ONLY place the decision lives, and so that turning it on\n      would be a deliberate edit here rather than a component nobody remembered to add.\n\n      And the script tag is never coming either, ID or no ID. Google's stock snippet fetches its\n      tag host and sets `_ga` on load, before any banner has been drawn, let alone answered; under\n      ePrivacy Art. 5(3) that is a violation a banner underneath does not cure. The one call site\n      that may inject it is the Accept button — see the header of `@cloudsforge/ui/consent`.\n    -->\n    <!--\n      Lantern carries `markId: null` in the registry (surfaces.ts), so no mark and no wordmark\n      appear anywhere in this bundle. These are the company chrome, which a separate hostname needs\n      regardless: a browser tab and a shared link inherit nothing.\n    -->\n    <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/png\" sizes=\"32x32\" href=\"/favicon-32x32.png\" />\n    <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/png\" sizes=\"192x192\" href=\"/favicon-192x192.png\" />\n    <link rel=\"apple-touch-icon\" sizes=\"192x192\" href=\"/favicon-192x192.png\" />\n\n    <title>Lantern — logs &amp; errors</title>\n\n    <!-- Relative on purpose: one bundle serves localhost, preview and the apex, and this file\n         names no hostname. Crawlers resolve it against the page they fetched. An absolute URL\n         here would bake a hostname into the artefact — the same defect as build-time config. -->\n    <meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"website\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Lantern\" />\n    <meta\n      property=\"og:description\"\n      content=\"The CloudsForge operator console for issues, events and browser samples.\"\n    />\n    <meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"/og-1200x630.png\" />\n    <meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\" />\n    <!-- An operator console has nothing a search engine should hold. `adminOnly: true`\n         (surfaces.ts) is the authorisation fact; this is the indexing one, and they are not\n         the same statement. -->\n    <meta name=\"robots\" content=\"noindex, nofollow\" />\n    <script type=\"module\" crossorigin src=\"/assets/index-C7-N2-P6.js\"></script>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" crossorigin href=\"/assets/index-BJcfEF3c.css\">\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\"root\"></div>\n  </body>\n</html>\n"
  },
